
<html>
<head><title> Kravchuk:Introduction Krawtchouk_Matrices </title></head>
<body>
<p>
<H2> Krawtchouk_Matrices </H2>
 
Kravchuk matrices?  You have seen them since your algebra classes in
high school.   
<p>
The reader is asked not to feel offended if we start from such a personal
experience as his/her first intimate encounter with the charm of
algebra concentrated around ``multiplication rules.''  Perhaps the first
vibes came at (a &plusmn;  b)<sup> 2 </sup>=a<sup> 2 </sup> &plusmn; 2ab+ b<sup> 2 </sup>.  Let us recall the first
amazement caused by vanishing middle terms in the product (a+b)(a-b).
These memories of the initiation to the mysteries of mathematics are
recalled now, for they are going to be developed on a deeper level and are basic
for our main subject.  The three rules
<p>
<table align=center><tr><td align=center> (a+b)<sup> 2 </sup>   <td align=right>=<td align=right>a<sup> 2 </sup>+2ab+b<sup> 2 </sup>   </tr>
<tr><td align=right>          (a+b)(a-b)<td align=right>=<td align=right>a<sup> 2 </sup>-b<sup> 2 </sup> </tr>
<tr><td align=right>            (a-b)<sup> 2 </sup>   <td align=right>=<td align=right>a<sup> 2 </sup>-2ab+b<sup> 2 </sup>  </tr>
<tr><td align=right></tr>
</table>
<p>
 can be coded into a single mnemotechnic matrix:
<p>
 <table border=4 align=center cellpadding=10 cellspacing=0><tr><td align=right>  1 <td align=right>  2 <td align=right>  1 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                 1 <td align=right>  0 <td align=right> -1 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                 1 <td align=right> -2 <td align=right>  1 </tr>
</table><p>
Believe it or not, we are looking at Kravchuk polynomials! Before we show it,
let us write the coefficients as columns and define a
``second-order Krawtchouk matrix'' as:
<p>
<sup> 2 </sup>K = 
<p>
<table border=4 align=center cellpadding=10 cellspacing=0><tr><td align=right>  1 <td align=right>  1 <td align=right>  1 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                             2 <td align=right>  0 <td align=right> -2 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                             1 <td align=right> -1 <td align=right>  1 </tr>
</table><p>
Similarly, the algebraic rules of degree three translate into a
``third-order Krawtchouk matrix'':
<p>
<table align=center><tr><td align=center> (a+b)<sup> 3 </sup>      <td align=right>=<td align=right> a<sup> 3 </sup>+3a<sup> 2 </sup>b+3ab<sup> 2 </sup>+b<sup> 3 </sup>  </tr>
<tr><td align=right>          (a+b)<sup> 2 </sup>(a-b) <td align=right>=<td align=right> a<sup> 3 </sup>+a<sup> 2 </sup>b-ab<sup> 2 </sup>-b<sup> 3 </sup>    </tr>
<tr><td align=right>          (a+b)(a-b)<sup> 2 </sup> <td align=right>=<td align=right> a<sup> 3 </sup>-a<sup> 2 </sup>b-ab<sup> 2 </sup>+b<sup> 3 </sup>    </tr>
<tr><td align=right>          (a-b)<sup> 2 </sup>      <td align=right>=<td align=right> a<sup> 3 </sup>-3a<sup> 2 </sup>b+3ab<sup> 2 </sup>+b<sup> 3 </sup> </tr>
</table>
<p>
are encoded in the matrix
<p>
<sup> 3 </sup>K =
<p>
       <table border=4 align=center cellpadding=10 cellspacing=0><tr><td align=right> 1 <td align=right>  1 <td align=right>  1  <td align=right>  1 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      3 <td align=right>  1 <td align=right> -1  <td align=right> -3 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      3 <td align=right> -1 <td align=right> -1  <td align=right>  3 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      1 <td align=right> -1 <td align=right>  1  <td align=right> -1 </tr>
</table><p>
Similarly, for order 4 one gets:
<p>
<sup> 4 </sup>K =
<p>
       <table border=4 align=center cellpadding=10 cellspacing=0><tr><td align=right> 1 <td align=right>  1 <td align=right>  1  <td align=right>  1  <td align=right>  1 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      4 <td align=right>  2 <td align=right>  0  <td align=right> -2  <td align=right> -4 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      6 <td align=right>  0 <td align=right> -2  <td align=right>  0  <td align=right>  6 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      4 <td align=right> -2 <td align=right>  0  <td align=right>  2  <td align=right> -4 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      1 <td align=right> -1 <td align=right>  1  <td align=right> -1  <td align=right>  1 </tr>
</table><p>
You may also include Krawtchouk matrices of order one and zero:
<p>
<sup> 0 </sup>K=
<p>
<table border=4 align=center cellpadding=10 cellspacing=0><tr><td align=right> 1 </tr>
</table> 
<p>
<sup> 1 </sup>K=
<p>
<table border=4 align=center cellpadding=10 cellspacing=0><tr><td align=right> 1 <td align=right>  1 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                          1 <td align=right> -1 </tr>
</table><p>
Thus, rewriting the algebraic rules and normalizing to a=1, b=v, one gets the
general definition of Krawtchouk matrices:
<p>
<dl>
<dt><b>Definition</b> <dd> 
The N<i>th</i>-degree Krawtchouk matrix <sup> N </sup>K is an (N+1)\times(N+1) matrix, the
rj entry defined by:
<p>
<table><tr><td align=center>    (1+v)<sup> i </sup> (1-v)<sup> j </sup> = 
<td align=center> N <br><b>Sum</b><br> r=0  <td>
<td>  <sup> N </sup>K<sub> rj </sub> v<sup> r </sup></tr>
</table> 
<p>
where N=i+j, and where the polynomial on the left-hand side
<p>
              G(v)= (1+v)<sup> i </sup> (1-v)<sup> j </sup>
<p>
can be viewed as a <i> generating function</i> for K.
</dl>
<p>
 
<p>
To recover the algebraic rules, one can use matrix multiplication <br>
<b> v</b>K where <b> v</b> = [1,v,v<sup> 2 </sup>, . . . ,v<sup> n </sup>].
 
<p>
Krawtchouk matrices have numerous beautiful symmetry properties
and satisfy many algebraic identities.  One remarkable identity (to be proven later)
concerns the entries of a single matrix:  <br> in any (sub)square of four adjacent
matrix entries, the three upper right numbers sum to the fourth, the
entry in the bottom left corner. This is called a ``square identity''. 
<p>
       <table border=4 align=center cellpadding=10 cellspacing=0><tr><td align=right> 1 <td align=right>  1 <td align=right>  1  <td align=right>  1  <td align=right>  1 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      4 <td align=right>  2 <td align=right>  0  <td align=right> -2  <td align=right> -4 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      6 <td align=right>  0 <td align=right> -2  <td align=right>  0  <td align=right>  6 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      4 <td align=right> -2 <td align=right>  0  <td align=right>  2  <td align=right> -4 </tr>
<tr><td align=right>                      1 <td align=right> -1 <td align=right>  1  <td align=right> -1  <td align=right>  1 </tr>
</table><p>
 
<tt> Verify the square identity: In any square of four cells, the left-bottom value is a sum of the other three numbers.</tt>
<p>
 
<b> Problems.</b>
<ul>
<li> Notice that the r<i> th</i> row of the matrix <sup> N-1 </sup>K may be recovered
from the (r-1)<i> st</i> row of the succeeding matrix <sup> N-1 </sup>K by subtraction.
<li> Averaging entries two-by-two along a row recovers the row of the previous matrix. 
<li> Subtracting a column of <sup> N </sup>K from its own copy
shifted up by one place reconstructs the next column over of the next Krawtchouk
matrix, <sup> N+1 </sup>K.  Explain this, starting from
<p>
(a+b)<sup> i </sup>(a-b)<sup> j+1 </sup>= (a-b) &times; (a+b)<sup> i </sup>(a-b)<sup> j </sup> .
<p>
Write this in matrix terms.
</ul>
 
<b> Problem.</b>  Pascal's triangle is clearly valid for the leftmost
columns, j=0.  Does it generalize for the other columns?
<p>
 
<b> Problem.</b>  Find a substitute for Pascal's triangle for rows.  
<p>
<b> Exercise.</b> Calculate the matrix powers K<sup> n </sup> for integer n &gt; 0.
<p>
The corresponding symmetric 
S-matrix is constructed from a K-matrix by multiplying each column by the 
corresponding binomial coefficient, K<sub> j0 </sub>. See the examples indicated below.
<p>
